(window.PHASE_DATA=window.PHASE_DATA||{})[6]={"pages": [{"pdf": 266, "printed": 239, "ch": 0, "text": "PHASE 6\n\nStress Testing and Climate-Financial\nModels\nChapters 35–42\n\nECB STAMP€; Brownlees and Engle (2017); NGFS Phase V; Dietz et al. (2016)"}, {"pdf": 267, "printed": 240, "ch": 35, "text": "CHAPTER 35\n\nPrerequisites: The Mathematics Behind\nStress Tests and SRISK\nSource line — read first\nThis chapter is my own preparation. It collects the tools used in Chapters 36–41: the difference between a stress and a risk measure, the probit transform, truncated-normal moments, a distributed-lag model and its long-run multiplier, GARCH volatility, and a capital-shortfall identity. Every result is proved here so that the later chapters can cite it. Nothing in this chapter comes from a single reading; the proofs are standard.\n\n35.1 Stress test versus risk measure\nA risk measure (Phase 5, Chapter 33) summarises an unconditional loss distribution: VaR0.999 is a quantile of L. A stress test instead fixes a scenario s (a path for GDP, rates, property prices, and for climate work, carbon prices and damages) and computes the conditional outcome E(CET1T | s). The two are linked by the law of total expectation, E(L) = P (s) E(L | s),\n\nso a stress test reports one term of this sum and says nothing about P (s). That is both its strength (transparent, narrative) and its weakness (no probability attached). SRISK (Chapter 39) is the bridge: a risk measure defined as a stress, E(CS | market falls ≥ 10%), with a probability implied by the event.\n\n35.2 The probit transform and distance to default\nFor a probability p ∈ (0, 1) define the distance to default D = −Φ−1 (p), so p = Φ(−D) (Chapter 28). The map p 7→ D is strictly decreasing and onto R. Result 35.1 (Bounded shifts). For any real ∆, p′ = Φ(−(D + ∆)) ∈ (0, 1), and p′ is strictly decreasing in ∆. For small ∆, p′ − p ≈ −φ(D)∆. Proof. Φ maps R onto (0, 1) and is strictly increasing; −(D + ∆) is strictly decreasing in ∆. A first-order Taylor expansion of Φ(−D − ∆) at ∆ = 0 gives derivative −φ(−D) = −φ(D). ■ In words: you can shift a bank’s risk by any amount in D and still have a valid probability. Multiplying p by a constant has no such guarantee: 3 × 40% = 120% is not a probability. This is why STAMP€ works in D (Chapter 37)."}, {"pdf": 268, "printed": 241, "ch": 35, "text": "35.3 Truncated-normal moments\nResult 35.2 (Mean of a truncated standard normal). For Z ∼ N (0, 1) and z ∈ R: E(Z | Z < z) = −φ(z)/Φ(z). More generally, for X ∼ N (µ, σ 2 ), a = (ℓ − µ)/σ, b = (u − µ)/σ: E X I{ℓ<X<u} = µ [Φ(b) − Φ(a)] + σ [φ(a) − φ(b)]. Proof. Since φ′ (u) = −uφ(u),\n\n−∞ uφ(u) du = −φ(z).\n\nDivide by P (Z < z) = Φ(z) for the\nfirst claim. For the second, write X = µ + σZ: E(XI) = µP (a < Z < b) + σ a uφ(u)du = µ[Φ(b) − Φ(a)] + σ[φ(a) − φ(b)].\n\nResult 35.3 (Capped expectation). Let S ∼ N (µ, σ 2 ) and 0 < L. Then, with a = −µ/σ, b = (L − µ)/σ, E min(max(S, 0), L) = µ[Φ(b) − Φ(a)] + σ[φ(a) − φ(b)] + L [1 − Φ(b)]. Proof. Split on {S ≤ 0} (value 0), {0 < S < L} (value S) and {S ≥ L} (value L). The middle piece is Theorem 35.2 with ℓ = 0, u = L; the last is L P (S ≥ L) = L[1 − Φ(b)]. This is exactly the structure of STAMP€’s “sales ratio” (Chapter 38).\n\n35.4 Distributed-lag models and the long-run multiplier\nAn ADL(p, q) model for a (transformed) default rate yt and a driver xt is\nyt = c +\n\nρi yt−i +\n\ni=1\n\nResult 35.4 (Long-run\nPq multiplier). If\nj=0 βj\nsatisfies ∂ ȳ/∂ x̄ =\n1 − pi=1 ρi\n\nβj xt−j + εt .\n\nj=0\n\nρi < 1 and xt ≡ x̄ is held constant, the steady state\n\nProof. Set yt = yt−i = ȳ and xt−j = x̄, ignore ε: ȳ(1 −\n\nρi ) = c + (\n\nβj )x̄. Differentiate. ■\n\nExample: ρ1 = 0.6, β0 = −0.10, β1 = −0.06 gives LRM = −0.16/0.4 = −0.40. A one-unit permanent rise in x lowers y by 0.40 in the long run, although the impact effect is only −0.10. Why the logit. If dt is a default rate in (0, 1), yt = ln 1−d is unbounded and the fitted dt = 1/(1 + e t ) always lies in (0, 1). A shift of ∆y multiplies the odds by e∆y .\n\n35.5 Counting models and Bayesian model averaging\nResult 35.5 (Number of equations). With K candidate regressors and at most L of them included in a model, the number of model specifications is I = L l=1 l ."}, {"pdf": 269, "printed": 242, "ch": 35, "text": "Proof. For each size l there are\n1, . . . , L.\n\nways to choose which regressors enter; sum over l =\n\nIf model m has estimated effect θ̂m and posterior weight wm ( wm = 1), the averaged effect is θ̂ = m wm θ̂m . Weights typically use a fit criterion (for example wm ∝ exp(− 21 BICm ) — my illustration; Chapter 37 says only that BIC is used for lag selection).\n\n35.6 GARCH volatility\nThe GARCH(1,1) model has returns rt = σt zt , zt i.i.d. with mean 0 and variance 1, and 2 . σt2 = ω + αrt−1 + βσt−1 Result 35.6 (Unconditional variance). If α + β < 1, the stationary variance is σ 2 = ω/(1 − α − β). 2 , and Er 2 Proof. Take expectations of the recursion: Eσt2 = ω + αErt−1 + βEσt−1 t−1 = Eσt−1 . In a stationary regime Eσt2 = σ 2 for all t, so σ 2 (1 − α − β) = ω.\n\nThe model creates volatility clustering: a large shock raises tomorrow’s variance. The GJR 2 I variant adds a term γrt−1 {rt−1 <0} so that falls raise volatility more than rises. In Chapter 39, the DCC extension lets the market–firm correlation ρit move over time.\n\n35.7 Capital shortfall as an accounting identity\nLet a bank have debt D, market equity W and assets A = D + W . With a prudential equity ratio k the capital shortfall is CS = kA − W = k(D + W ) − W = kD − (1 − k)W. If equity falls by a fraction R (so W → W (1 − R)) and debt is unchanged, CS = kD − (1 − k)W (1 − R). This is Chapter 39’s starting point; the leverage form is CS = W [k LVG − 1] with LVG = (D + W )/W before the fall.\n\n35.8 Conditional expectation given an event, by Monte Carlo\nTo estimate E(X | A) from S simulated pairs (X s , As ) use the ratio estimator Ê = P s \u000eP s X IAs s IAs . It is consistent when P (A) > 0, but only about S P (A) draws carry information. If P (A) = 1.2% and S = 104 , about 120 draws are used and the standard error is large — a concern that motivates the computational shortcut in Brownlees and Engle’s Appendix A, which draws market paths first and then selects (Chapter 39)."}, {"pdf": 270, "printed": 243, "ch": 35, "text": "Check yourself\nRecall\nQ35.1 State Theorem 35.2 and Theorem 35.4.\nQ35.2 Write CS in debt–equity form and leverage form.\nExplain\nQ35.3 Why is a stress-test number not a risk measure?\nQ35.4 Why does shifting in D keep PDs inside [0, 1]?\nApply\nQ35.5 p = 2%, ∆ = −0.5. Compute p′ .\nQ35.6 K = 10, L = 4. How many equations?\nQ35.7 ω = 2 × 10−6 , α = 0.07, β = 0.91. Find the long-run daily volatility.\nViva\nQ35.8 Prove E(Z | Z < z) = −φ(z)/Φ(z).\nQ35.9 Explain why a large permanent shock to x has a bigger effect on y in the long run than at impact.\n\nAnswer sketches\nA35.1 E(Z | Z < z) = −φ(z)/Φ(z); LRM =\n\nβj /(1 −\n\nρi ).\n\nA35.2 CS = kD − (1 − k)W = W (k LVG − 1).\nA35.3 It is one conditional term in E(L) =\nP (s)E(L | s); P (s) is not given.\nA35.4 Φ maps R onto (0, 1), so any real shift gives a valid probability.\nA35.5 D = 2.054; ∆ = −0.5 gives D′ = 1.554 and p′ = Φ(−1.554) = 6.0%.\nA35.6 10\n1 + 2 + 3 + 4 = 10 + 45 + 120 + 210 = 385.\nA35.7 σ 2 = 2 × 10−6 /0.02 = 10−4 , so σ = 1% per day.\nA35.8 −∞ uφ(u)du = −φ(z) since φ′ = −uφ; divide by Φ(z).\nA35.9 The lagged dependent variable propagates the effect: 1/(1 − ρ) > 1 when 0 < ρ < 1 and β’s have one sign."}, {"pdf": 271, "printed": 244, "ch": 36, "text": "CHAPTER 36\n\nSTAMP€: A Macro Stress Test as Four\nLinked Modules\nSource line — read first\nSource actually read: the ECB’s STAMP€: Stress-Test Analytics for Macroprudential Purposes in the euro area (ECB report; the text in the folder is long, about 13 000 extracted lines). I read the framework chapter (printed p. 15 gives the four-pillar figure), the application chapter (Chapter 3, the 2016 EU-wide exercise) and the credit-risk satellite chapter (Chapter 4). I did not read Chapters 5–13 in detail (loan-flow models, DSGE and GVAR, interbank networks); Section 36.4 below is from the framework overview only. Numbers quoted from Chapter 3 are as I read them from the extracted text. The report contains no climate content; the climate links in these notes are my inference.\n\n36.1 Why a stress-testing framework belongs in a climate-risk thesis\nYour thesis asks how climate transition risk affects the capital adequacy of Indian public sector banks. Capital adequacy is a balance-sheet concept: loans default, provisions rise, profits fall, and capital over risk-weighted assets moves. A stress-test framework is the machine that turns an assumed macro-financial path into that capital ratio. STAMP€ is a fully documented example from a central bank, so it shows what the pieces are and where the climate scenario would plug in.\n\n36.2 The four pillars\n1. Scenario design. A baseline and an adverse path for macro-financial variables (GDP, unemployment, interest rates, house prices, equity prices, exchange rates).\n2. Satellite models. Each risk driver gets its own model, estimated on data and driven by the scenario: probability of default (PD), loss given default (LGD), bank interest-rate spreads, net interest margin (NIM), market risk, fee income and operational risk.\n3. Balance-sheet module. Accounting identities: loans, non-performing loans (NPL), provisions, risk-weighted assets (RWA), profit, and the capital ratio.\n4. Feedback modules. Banks react and the real economy reacts back (Section 36.4)."}, {"pdf": 272, "printed": 245, "ch": 36, "text": "1. Scenario\ndesign\n\n2. Satellite\nmodels\n\n3. Balance-sheet\nmodule\n\n4. Feedback\nmodules\n\nmacro and financial\npaths (baseline,\nadverse)\n\nPD, LGD, NIM,\nspreads, market,\nfees, op. risk\n\nloans, NPLs,\nprovisions, RWA,\nCET1\n\nbank deleveraging,\ncontagion, DSGE/GVAR,\nflow of funds\n\nfeedback to the scenario (macro and credit supply)\n\nFigure 36.1. The STAMP€ architecture (framework figure, report p. 15), redrawn in my words. Scenarios feed satellite models for each risk driver, which feed a balance-sheet module that computes capital; feedback modules return the bank’s reaction to the macro economy. Satellite\n\nWhat it does in a bank stress test Climate analogue (my inference)\n\nPD (Ch. 37)\n\nScenario → default rate by portfolio seg- Sector-level PD uplift\nment\nintensive borrowers\n\nLGD (Ch. 38)\n\nCollateral value → loss if default\n\nValue of stranded or flood-exposed collateral\n\nSpreads and NIM\n\nFunding costs and margin\n\nRisk premia on brown assets\n\nMarket risk\n\nRevaluation of securities\n\nRepricing of equity and bonds of fossilheavy issuers\n\nFees,\nrisk\n\noperational Non-interest income and losses\n\nfor\n\ncarbon-\n\nPhysical damage to bank premises\n(second-order)\n\nTable 36.1. STAMP€ satellite models and their climate analogues.\n\n36.3 The application: the 2016 EU-wide exercise (Chapter 3)\nChapter 3 applies the framework to the 2016 EU-wide stress test, using 37 banks. As I read the extracted text: How to read the contagion line. Contagion is small relative to the 390 bp fall: the direct credit and income channels dominate; interbank spillovers matter in the tail (90th percentile is twice the median).\n\n36.4 Feedback modules: what the overview says\nThe framework lists several feedback channels. I list them with their role, from the overview only. Dynamic balance sheet and loan flows. Credit supply responds to capital: weaker capital → less lending → lower GDP."}, {"pdf": 273, "printed": 246, "ch": 36, "text": "Quantity\n\nReported result (as I read it)\n\nBanks\n\nCET1 ratio, start → end of adverse scenario 13.0% → 9.1%, a fall of about 390 bp Corporate lending vs baseline after 3 years\n\nAbout 15% lower\n\nHousehold lending vs baseline after 3 years\n\nAbout 9% lower\n\nGDP effect of the deleveraging feedback\n\n0.2–0.5% (6% capital target) and 0.3–0.8% (8% target);\nmy reading — verify\n\nInterbank contagion (loss of CET1)\n\n6 bp at the median bank, 12 bp at the 90th percentile\n\nTable 36.2. Headline results of the STAMP€ application (Chapter 3).\n\nOptimising deleveraging (Hałaj). The bank chooses how to shrink the balance sheet to meet a capital target, rather than shrinking proportionally. DSGE and GVAR macro feedback. Two macro-model families return the bank-lending shock to GDP. Interbank contagion and fire sales. Losses and forced asset sales transmit stress between banks. Flow-of-funds cross-sector spillovers. A network of sector-to-sector exposures reconstructed by maximum-entropy methods. Do you need the original? For the architecture and the headline numbers: no. Use this chapter when you describe the framework. Open the report (Chapter 3, the results tables) before quoting any figure in Table 36.2, particularly the GDP ranges. Open Chapters 5–13 if your thesis models credit-supply feedback; I did not study them.\n\n36.5 What it means for the thesis (my inference)\n1. A climate stress test is an ordinary stress test with a different scenario module. The satellite and balance-sheet pillars carry over; what changes is how sectors are shocked (Chapter 40).\n2. Partial equilibrium is the realistic choice for a doctoral thesis. The feedback pillar needs a macro model and a bank-behaviour model. A thesis can state that feedback is omitted and that the result is therefore a first-round estimate.\n3. Report the decomposition. Table 36.2 shows the habit to copy: total effect, then a separate line for contagion.\n\nCheck yourself\nRecall\nQ36.1 Name the four pillars and the seven satellite models.\nQ36.2 What happened to the CET1 ratio in the 2016 application?"}, {"pdf": 274, "printed": 247, "ch": 36, "text": "Explain\nQ36.3 Why does a stress test need a balance-sheet module as well as a PD model?\nQ36.4 Why is contagion shown separately from the direct effect?\nApply\nQ36.5 A bank starts at 13.0% CET1 and falls by 390 bp. What is the end ratio, and by what proportion of the starting ratio did it fall?\nQ36.6 Which pillar would you change to introduce a carbon tax, and which one stays the same? Viva\nQ36.7 The report contains no climate scenario. Defend the claim that it is still useful for a climate-risk thesis.\nQ36.8 What can a partial-equilibrium stress test not tell you?\n\nAnswer sketches\nA36.1 Scenario design; satellite models; balance sheet; feedback. Satellites: PD, LGD, spreads, NIM, market risk, fees, operational risk.\nA36.2 From 13.0% to 9.1%, about 390 bp, for 37 banks.\nA36.3 PDs are not capital; losses must pass through provisions, profit, RWA and retained earnings.\nA36.4 The direct channel is a first-round effect; contagion is conditional on bank interlinkages and has a fat tail (median 6 bp vs 12 bp at the 90th percentile).\nA36.5 13.0 − 3.9 = 9.1%; 3.9/13.0 = 30%.\nA36.6 The scenario module (pillar 1); the balance-sheet identities (pillar 3) are unchanged.\nA36.7 The pillars are generic: scenario → risk drivers → capital. A climate scenario is another input to pillar 1.\nA36.8 It cannot say how banks’ deleveraging changes GDP, or how losses spread between banks."}, {"pdf": 275, "printed": 248, "ch": 37, "text": "CHAPTER 37\n\nProbability of Default in STAMP€: Model\nAveraging and the Distance-to-Default\nShift\nSource line — read first\nSource actually read: STAMP€ Chapter 4, sections on the PD satellite models\n(extracted-text lines about 2 493–3 400 of the folder file). Equation forms are the report’s; proofs and the numerical tables are mine. I did not check the report’s estimated coefficients or its sign-restriction table (Table 4.1) line by line, and the exact lag rules are summarised from my reading.\n\n37.1 The problem\nA stress test needs a default rate for each portfolio segment under each scenario. History gives short samples and many candidate macro drivers: a recipe for over-fitting. STAMP€ responds with (i) many small distributed-lag models, (ii) an average over them, (iii) sign restrictions to keep economic sense, and (iv) a rule to carry sector PDs to bank PDs.\n\n37.2 Segments and data\nSix segments: non-financial corporates in real estate (NFC-RE) and not in real estate (NFC non-RE); households for house purchase (HH-HP) and consumer credit (HH-CC); financial institutions (FIN); sovereigns (SOV). For financials the report uses Moody’s KMV default probabilities (the Merton-type, physical measure of Chapter 28); for sovereigns it uses Kamakura. The text prefers real-world to CDS-implied PDs, which is the p-versus-q point of Chapter 29.\n\n37.3 The model: logit ADL averaged over many equations\n. For each segment the\nThe dependent variable is the logit of the default rate, yt = ln 1−d\nreport estimates an ADL model of the type in Chapter 35, with lags chosen by BIC and with “closed lag structures” (my reading: if a lag of a variable enters, all shorter lags enter).\n\nModel space. With K candidate drivers and at most L drivers in a model, the number of equations is I = L l=1 l (Theorem 35.5)."}, {"pdf": 276, "printed": 249, "ch": 37, "text": "K (candidates) L (max in model) I (equations)\n\n385\n\n793\n\nTable 37.1. Size of the model space (Theorem 35.5); my computation. The report’s own K and L are not reproduced here.\n\nLong-run multipliers. Each model’s response to a sustained shock is summarised by the long-run multiplier LRM = (β0 + · · · + βq )/(1 − ρi ) (Theorem 35.4). The report also reports normalised LRMs, multiplied by sd(X)/sd(Y ), so that effects of different drivers can be compared in units of standard deviations of the default series. Sign restrictions (Table 4.1 of the report) discard any model whose LRM for a driver has an economically wrong sign (for example, higher unemployment lowering defaults). The surviving models are combined with posterior weights. Why average? No single specification is believed. If the models give different θ̂m , the average has lower variance than a randomly chosen model, and it does not hide the dispersion: the spread of θ̂m across the model space is a measure of model risk (my inference).\n\n37.4 Attaching a bank to a sector PD: the distance-to-default rule\nThe scenario produces a sector PD path. A bank has its own starting PD p0 . The report’s rule: sec −1 sec\n1. D0 = −Φ−1 (p0 ) for the bank; D0sec = −Φ−1 (psec\n0 ) and Dt = −Φ (pt ) for the sector.\n\n2. ∆Dt = Dtsec − D0sec .\n\n3. Bank PD pt = Φ − (D0 + ∆Dt ) .\nBy Theorem 35.1 this lies in (0, 1), rises with the scenario, and does not require pt ≤ 1 to be imposed. Worked example. The sector PD rises from 1% to 3%. Then D0sec = 2.326, D1sec = 1.881, ∆D = −0.446. Table 37.2 applies this to banks with different starting PDs. Result 37.1 (Behaviour of the D-shift). For a fixed ∆D < 0, the ratio pt /p0 is decreasing in p0 and tends to ∞ as p0 → 0; the absolute increase pt − p0 is largest for a bank whose starting D0 equals |∆D|/2 (a starting PD just below 50%). Proof. Write D0 = −Φ−1 (p0 ) and g(D) = Φ(−D − ∆D)/Φ(−D). For large D, using Φ(−x) ∼ φ(x)/x, g(D) ≈ exp(−D ∆D − ∆D2 /2) · D/(D + ∆D) → ∞ (as ∆D < 0, the exponent is positive and grows with D). For the second claim, pt − p0 = D00+∆D φ(u) du is the normal"}, {"pdf": 277, "printed": 250, "ch": 37, "text": "Start PD (%)\n\nD0 Stressed PD, D-shift (%) Stressed PD, ×3 (%)\n\n0.1 3.090\n\n0.41\n\n0.30\n\n0.5 2.576\n\n1.66\n\n1.50\n\n1.0 2.326\n\n3.00\n\n3.00\n\n2.0 2.054\n\n5.39\n\n6.00\n\n5.0 1.645\n\n11.52\n\n15.00\n\n50.0\n\n67.20\n\n150 (invalid)\n\nTable 37.2. Bank PD under the sector shock 1% → 3%: the distance-to-default rule against a naive tripling. My computation.\n\nStressed PD (%)\n\nStarting PD\nNaive: multiply by 3\nDistance-to-default shift (ΔD = −0.446)\n\n0.1%\n\n0.5%\n\n1.0%\nBank's starting PD\n\n2.0%\n\n5.0%\n\nFigure 37.1. Stressed bank PD for the sector shock in Table 37.2. A bank that is already weaker than the sector ends up with a smaller proportional increase than the naive multiple, and a high-quality bank with a larger one.\n\ndensity integrated over an interval of fixed length |∆D|, which is largest when the interval is centred at zero, i.e. D0 = |∆D|/2. Reading: the rule is multiplicative in the tails and additive in the middle; it matches how structural default models behave (Chapter 28). The report’s text does not prove this; the result and the table are mine. Do you need the original? For the modelling logic and the D-shift: no. Open Chapter 4 (the sign-restriction table, the segment-specific regressors and the weighting rule) if you are going to replicate or cite the specifications. I did not extract the report’s coefficient tables.\n\n37.5 What it means for the thesis (my inference)\n1. Your sector shock is the scenario output. A climate scenario that raises the PD of carbon-intensive sectors from, say, 2% to 4% generates a ∆D; apply it to each bank’s starting D (the pipeline of Chapter 42).\n2. Indian public sector banks lack KMV-type PDs for most borrowers. The"}, {"pdf": 278, "printed": 251, "ch": 37, "text": "Bank PD (%)\n\n102\n\n101\n\n100\nstart PD 0.5%\nstart PD 5.0%\nstart PD 30.0%\n\n10−1\n−1.25\n\n−1.00\n\n−0.75\n−0.50\n−0.25\n0.00\nChange in distance to default ΔD\n\n0.25\n\n0.50\n\nFigure 37.2. Bank PD as a function of the shift ∆D, for three starting PDs (log scale). The curves are parallel in the tails and all lie in (0, 1).\n\nD-shift needs a starting PD for the bank’s portfolio, which could be an NPL-based or rating-based estimate. That input is an assumption in your thesis.\n3. Model averaging is a defensive device. Examiners will ask why you chose a specification; an average with sign restrictions answers it, at the price of heavier computation.\n\nCheck yourself\nRecall\nQ37.1 List the six PD segments and the data source for financials and sovereigns.\nQ37.2 Define the long-run multiplier and the normalised LRM.\nExplain\nQ37.3 Why a logit for the default rate?\nQ37.4 Why impose sign restrictions after estimating?\nApply\nQ37.5 A bank has p0 = 3% and the sector shock is 2% → 5%. Find the stressed bank PD.\nQ37.6 K = 7, L = 3. How many equations?\nQ37.7 ρ = 0.5, β0 = 0.2, β1 = 0.1. Find the LRM.\nViva\nQ37.8 Prove that the D-shift cannot produce a PD above one.\nQ37.9 Give two limitations of using sector-level PDs to stress a single bank.\n\nAnswer sketches\nA37.1 NFC-RE, NFC non-RE, HH-HP, HH-CC, FIN, SOV; Moody’s KMV for financials, Kamakura for sovereigns."}, {"pdf": 279, "printed": 252, "ch": 37, "text": "A37.2\n\nβj /(1 −\n\nρi ); multiplied by sd(X)/sd(Y ).\n\nA37.3 It maps (0, 1) to R, so fitted rates lie in (0, 1).\nA37.4 Many specifications fit the short sample; restrictions remove those with implausible signs.\nA37.5 ∆D = −Φ−1 (0.05) + Φ−1 (0.02) = 1.645 − 2.054 = −0.409. D0 = 1.881; D1 = 1.472; p1 = Φ(−1.472) = 7.1%.\nA37.6 7 + 21 + 35 = 63.\nA37.7 0.3/0.5 = 0.6.\nA37.8 Φ takes values in (0, 1) for every real argument.\nA37.9 The sector PD hides the bank’s composition; the shift is the same for banks with very different portfolios; the starting PD is only a proxy."}, {"pdf": 280, "printed": 253, "ch": 38, "text": "CHAPTER 38\n\nFrom PD to Balance Sheet: NPL Dynamics and the Structural LGD\nSource line — read first\nSource actually read: STAMP€ Chapter 4, the NPL and performing-loan accounting equations, and the section “2 Loss given default (LGD) model” (printed p. 53 of the report, running footer “Chapter 4 Credit risk satellite models”). The LGD formula and the sales-ratio expression are the report’s. The extracted text does not state the loss-given-liquidation (LGL) formula explicitly; the step LGL = 1 − SR/LT V is my reading (Section 38.2.1) and is flagged. The numerical tables and their checks are mine.\n\n38.1 NPL and performing-loan accounting\nLet Nt be non-performing and Pt performing loans. The report’s recursions, which I restate with names: Nt = Nt−1 (1 − wt ) + PDt Pt−1 − Curet ,\n\n(38.1)\n\nPt = Pt−1 (1 − rt − PDt ) + NBt + Curet .\n\n(38.2)\n\nHere wt is the write-off rate, rt the repayment rate, NBt new business, and Curet the NPLs returning to performing. The two equations are stock-flow identities: new NPLs (PDt Pt−1 ) leave the performing book and enter the NPL book; cures move the other way. Worked example (mine). Total loans 100: N0 = 8, P0 = 92. Write-off rate w = 5%, cure = 2% of Nt−1 , new business NBt = r Pt−1 (new lending exactly replaces repayments). Then Pt = Pt−1 (1 − PDt ) + Curet . Compare a baseline (PD 2% every year) with a stress (PD 4%, 7%, 5%). Year 0 Year 1 Year 2 Year 3 Baseline NPL stock\n\n8.00\n\n9.28\n\n10.44\n\n11.48\n\nBaseline NPL ratio (%)\n\n8.0\n\n9.3\n\n10.5\n\n11.6\n\nStress NPL stock\n\n8.00\n\n11.12\n\n16.54\n\n19.50\n\nStress NPL ratio (%)\n\n8.0\n\n11.2\n\n16.7\n\n19.9\n\nTable 38.1. NPL dynamics under baseline and stress PD paths. My computation from the recursions above."}, {"pdf": 281, "printed": 254, "ch": 38, "text": "Baseline PD 2%/yr\nStress PD 4%, 7%, 5%\n\nNPL ratio (%)\n\nYear\n\nFigure 38.1. NPL ratio under the two PD paths of Table 38.1. Even the baseline rises because the write-off and cure rates are lower than the default inflow (a feature of my parameters).\n\nRead the stock, not the flow. A one-year PD spike of 7% leaves an NPL ratio that stays high for years because write-offs and cures are slow. That persistence is why loss estimates over a three-year horizon exceed the first-year loss.\n\n38.2 The structural LGD\nFor housing-related portfolios the report links LGD to property prices:\nLGD = (1 − P (cure)) · LGL + Costs.\nCure is the chance that a defaulted borrower returns to performing without liquidation; LGL is the loss if the collateral is liquidated; Costs are administrative costs.\n\n38.2.1 The sales-ratio expression: derivation\nThe report assumes the sales ratio S (the price obtained relative to the current collateral value) is normal with mean µ and standard deviation σ, and that the recovery is capped at the loan. Measure everything in units of collateral value: the loan is LTV and the recovery is min(S, LTV) (and not below zero). By Theorem 35.3, SR = E[min(max(S, 0), LTV)] = µ[Φ(b) − Φ(a)] + σ[φ(a) − φ(b)] + LTV [1 − Φ(b)], with a = −µ/σ and b = (LTV − µ)/σ. This is the report’s expression, whose exponential terms are √σ2π e−µ /2σ − e−(LTV−µ) /2σ . My reading of LGL. The loss per unit of loan is the shortfall of the capped recovery relative to the loan: LGL = 1 − SR/LTV. The report says only that LGL “can be derived from the loan-to-value ratio and the assumed sales ratio”. Numerical check. µ = 0.90, σ = 0.20, LTV = 0.70: the closed form gives SR = 0.68334; a Monte Carlo average of min(max(S, 0), 0.7) over 4 × 106 draws gives 0.68337. The formula and the simulation agree."}, {"pdf": 282, "printed": 255, "ch": 38, "text": "38.2.2 Indexing the LTV to house prices\nWith a house-price index H, the collateral value moves with H(t) while the loan stays fixed, so LTV(t) = LTV(0)\n\nH(0)\nH(t)\n\nA 20% price fall raises LTV by a factor 1/0.8 = 1.25. The report notes two timing options: indexing year by year, or “front-loading” the cumulative price change into the first year (perfect foresight of prices). Example calibration (the report’s illustrative numbers). Cure rate 40% (falling under stress), costs 5%, σ = 20%. I add µ = 0.90 and a starting LTV of 0.60 (my choices); cure falls to 30% under stress. House-price change (%) Indexed LTV LGL (%) Cure (%) LGD (%)\n\n0.600\n\n0.98\n\n5.59\n\n−10\n\n0.667\n\n1.80\n\n6.26\n\n−20\n\n0.750\n\n3.50\n\n7.45\n\n−30\n\n0.857\n\n7.02\n\n9.92\n\n−40\n\n1.000\n\n13.96\n\n14.77\n\nTable 38.2. Structural LGD as house prices fall. My computation with the parameters stated above; LGL uses 1 − SR/LTV. LGL, cure effects excluded\n\nLGD with cure 40% → 30% and costs 5%\n\nLGD (%)\n\nLoss given liquidation (%)\n\nHouse-price fall (%)\n\nHouse-price fall (%)\n\nFigure 38.2. Left: loss given liquidation as a function of the house-price fall. Right: LGD at the points of Table 38.2.\n\nResult 38.1 (Convexity of LGL in LTV). For S ∼ N (µ, σ 2 ) and x > 0, define SR(x) = E[min(max(S, 0), x)]. Then SR′ (x) = 1 − Φ (x − µ)/σ is decreasing, so SR is concave in x and x − SR(x) (the expected shortfall in collateral units) is convex. Proof. min(max(S, 0), x) = 0 I{S>u} du for S > 0, so SR(x) = 0 P (S > u) du and SR′ (x) = P (S > x) = 1 − Φ((x − µ)/σ), decreasing in x. In words: LGD rises slowly while LTV is low and then accelerates, as Table 38.2 and the left panel of Figure 38.2 show."}, {"pdf": 283, "printed": 256, "ch": 38, "text": "Other segments. For non-real-estate segments (NFC non-RE, HH-CC) a fixed multiplier is applied to the starting LGD; an alternative based on the PD–LGD correlation is mentioned but described as hard to justify empirically; sovereign LGD is fixed, for example 40%. Do you need the original? For the accounting recursions and the structural LGD logic: no. Open the report’s Chapter 4 (printed p. 53) to see the exact way LGL is built from the sales ratio, because the extracted text does not display it.\n\n38.3 What it means for the thesis (my inference)\n1. LGD is where physical-risk channels enter. A flood or heat event that lowers the value of mortgaged property raises LTV and hence LGD through exactly this mechanism.\n2. Transition risk acts through collateral too. Stranded-asset collateral loses value; the indexing rule LTV(t) = LTV(0)H(0)/H(t) can use a sector asset-price index in place of a house-price index.\n3. Indian PSB data. Reported NPLs, write-offs and recoveries (including recoveries under resolution frameworks) can calibrate w, Cure and Costs. That calibration is an empirical task for you.\n\nCheck yourself\nRecall\nQ38.1 Write the NPL and performing-loan recursions and interpret each term.\nQ38.2 State the structural LGD formula.\nExplain\nQ38.3 Why is LGD higher when LTV is higher?\nQ38.4 What is the difference between indexing and front-loading house prices?\nApply\nQ38.5 N0 = 8, P0 = 92, w = 5%, Cure = 2%, PD = 4%. Find N1 .\nQ38.6 A 25% price fall; starting LTV 0.60. What is the indexed LTV?\nQ38.7 Compute LGD with LGL = 7%, cure 30%, costs 5%.\nViva\nQ38.8 Derive the sales-ratio expression from the normal distribution.\nQ38.9 Why can LGD stay low for moderate price falls and then rise sharply?"}, {"pdf": 284, "printed": 257, "ch": 38, "text": "Answer sketches\nA38.1 Nt = Nt−1 (1 − w) + PDPt−1 − Cure; Pt = Pt−1 (1 − r − PD) + NB + Cure. New NPLs leave the performing book; cures return.\nA38.2 LGD = (1 − P (cure)) LGL + Costs.\nA38.3 The loan exceeds a larger share of the collateral value, so recoveries are capped below the loan more often.\nA38.4 Indexing moves LTV with the price path each year; front-loading applies the whole cumulative fall at once.\nA38.5 N1 = 8(0.95) + 0.04(92) − 0.16 = 7.6 + 3.68 − 0.16 = 11.12.\nA38.6 0.60/0.75 = 0.80.\nA38.7 0.7 × 0.07 + 0.05 = 0.099, i.e. 9.9%.\nA38.8 Theorem 35.3: split on S ≤ 0, 0 < S < LTV, S ≥ LTV; use Theorem 35.2.\nA38.9 SR′ (x) decreases in x: only when LTV nears the centre of the sales-ratio distribution does extra LTV start losing recovery (Theorem 38.1)."}, {"pdf": 285, "printed": 258, "ch": 39, "text": "CHAPTER 39\n\nSRISK: The Expected Capital Shortfall\nof a Bank in a Market Crash (Brownlees\nand Engle, 2017)\nSource line — read first\nSource actually read: Brownlees, C. and Engle, R. F. (2017), “SRISK: A conditional capital shortfall measure of systemic risk”, Review of Financial Studies 30(1): 48–79. I read the abstract, the introduction, the definition and estimation sections, Section 2 on data, and Appendix A (the LRMES simulation algorithm, pp. 75–76). I did not read the empirical prediction sections (rankings, the aggregate-SRISK regressions, Fed-injection tests) or Appendix B (time-varying copula) in full; the empirical claims below are the authors’ abstract and introduction. Formulas (1)–(4) are the paper’s; the derivations, the tables and the numerical checks are mine.\n\n39.1 The idea in a paragraph\nMost systemic-risk measures ask how a firm and the market co-move. SRISK asks a balancesheet question: if the market falls by a large amount over the next month, how much capital would this bank be short of ? The answer combines three ingredients: size and leverage (from the balance sheet), and the expected equity loss in a crash (from return data). Banks that are large, highly leveraged and sensitive to market falls have a large SRISK (p. 57 of the paper describes a crash horizon of one month).\n\n39.2 Definition and derivation\nLet D be debt (book value), W the market value of equity, k the prudential capital ratio (the paper uses 8%). Chapter 35 gave CS = k(D + W ) − W = kD − (1 − k)W . The systemic event is a fall of the market below a threshold C (the paper uses −10%) over a horizon h (one month, 22 trading days). Define the long-run marginal expected shortfall LRMESi = −E Ri,t+1:t+h | Rm,t+1:t+h < C , the expected equity loss in the crash. Result 39.1 (SRISK, the paper’s formula (1)). If debt is not renegotiated in the event and equity falls by LRMESi , SRISKi = E(CSi | crash) = kDi − (1 − k)Wi (1 − LRMESi ) = Wi k LVGi + (1 − k)LRMESi − 1 , with LVGi = (Di + Wi )/Wi ."}, {"pdf": 286, "printed": 259, "ch": 39, "text": "Proof. In the crash, equity becomes Wi (1−R) with E(R | crash) = LRMESi and Di unchanged. By linearity of expectation, E(CSi | crash) = kDi − (1 − k)Wi (1 − LRMESi ). For the second form substitute Di = Wi (LVGi − 1): kWi (LVGi − 1) − (1 − k)Wi + (1 − k)Wi LRMESi = Wi [k LVGi − k − 1 + k + (1 − k)LRMESi ]. Reading the formula SRISK is positive when k LVG + (1 − k)LRMES > 1. With k = 8% and LRMES = 15%: positive when LVG > (1 − 0.138)/0.08 = 10.8. Capital shortfall is mainly a leverage phenomenon.\n\nSRISK as a share of equity W\n\nLRMES 10%\nLRMES 20%\nLRMES 30%\n\nLeverage LVG = (D+W)/W\n\nFigure 39.1. SRISK per unit of equity as a function of leverage for three values of LRMES (Theorem 39.1). My plot.\n\nAggregation. The paper sums positive contributions: SRISK =\ni SRISKi , with\nSRISK%i = SRISKi /SRISK. A firm with excess capital does not offset another firm’s shortfall (the deficit of one cannot be used to recapitalise another).\n\n39.2.1 Worked example\nThree banks (currency units are arbitrary, say Rs thousand crore), k = 8%, market daily volatility 1.2%, h = 22, C = −10%: Bank\n\nβ LVG LRMES (%) SRISK Share (%)\n\n2.0 1.0\n\n26.0\n\n12.7\n\n2.39\n\n3.5\n\n200\n\n5.0 1.4\n\n41.0\n\n17.8\n\n12.22\n\n18.1\n\n800 15.0 1.6\n\n54.3\n\n20.4\n\n53.01\n\n78.4\n\n67.62\n\n100\n\nTotal\n\nTable 39.1. SRISK for three hypothetical banks. LRMES from the static normal formula of Theorem 39.2; SRISK computed by the debt–equity form and checked against the leverage form (identical). My illustration; the banks are invented.\n\nBank C has three times B’s equity but 4.3 times B’s SRISK (53.0 against 12.2), because its debt is four times larger, its leverage is higher and its beta is higher. Bank A’s SRISK (2.39)"}, {"pdf": 287, "printed": 260, "ch": 39, "text": "exceeds its equity (2.0): in a crash the shortfall is larger than the whole equity cushion.\n\n39.3 Estimating LRMES\n39.3.1 The static approximation\nResult 39.2 (Static bivariate-normal LRMES). Suppose the h-period log returns satisfy ri = βrm + ε, with rm ∼ N (0, σh2 ), ε independent of rm with mean 0. If the crash is rm < c (with c = ln(1 + C)), then E(ri | rm < c) = −β σh\n\nφ(c/σh )\nΦ(c/σh )\n\nσh = σd h.\n\nConsequently the LRMES approximated with log returns is β σh φ(z)/Φ(z), z = c/σh . Proof. E(ri | rm < c) = βE(rm | rm < c) + E(ε), and E(rm | rm < c) = σh E(Z | Z < z) = −σh φ(z)/Φ(z) by Theorem 35.2. The paper’s equation (4) is a closed form of this type, built from the bivariate normal; my derivation assumes zero mean and log returns, so it is an approximation to the arithmetic return the paper defines. β Static formula (%) Monte Carlo, arithmetic (%) P (crash) (%) 0.8\n\n9.73\n\n9.16\n\n1.23\n\n1.0\n\n12.16\n\n11.27\n\n1.24\n\n1.5\n\n18.24\n\n16.53\n\n1.23\n\n2.0\n\n24.33\n\n21.43\n\n1.23\n\nTable 39.2. LRMES by the static formula and by Monte Carlo (2 × 106 draws, arithmetic returns, daily market volatility 1%, daily idiosyncratic volatility 1.2%, h = 22). My computation. The static log-return formula overstates the arithmetic LRMES, more so for high β. 25.0\n\nStatic normal formula\n\nMonte Carlo, arithmetic returns\n\nLRMES (%) at beta 1\n\n22.5\n\nLRMES (%)\n\n20.0\n17.5\n15.0\n12.5\n\n10.0\n0.8\n\n1.0\n\n1.2\n\n1.4\n1.6\nFirm beta\n\n1.8\n\n2.0\n\n1.0\n\n1.5\n2.0\n2.5\nMarket daily volatility (%)\n\nFigure 39.2. Left: LRMES against beta, static versus Monte Carlo. Right: LRMES at β = 1 as market volatility increases. The measure is highly sensitive to volatility.\n\n260\n\n3.0"}, {"pdf": 288, "printed": 261, "ch": 39, "text": "39.3.2 The GARCH–DCC simulation (Appendix A)\nThe dynamic estimator in Appendix A, in my words. The parameters are treated as known.\n1. From the fitted GARCH–DCC\nmodel construct standardised innovations ϵmt = rmt /σmt\n\u0001 q\nand ξit = rit /σit − ρit ϵmt / 1 − ρ2it . They have mean zero, variance one and are uncorrelated.\n2. Sample with replacement S × h pairs (ξit , ϵmt ) and arrange them into S pseudo-samples for days T + 1, . . . , T + h.\n3. Feed the pseudo-innovations through the DCC and GARCH filters from the last observed values, to get S paths of log returns. P s\n4. Convert to multi-period arithmetic returns Ri,T\nt ri,T +t − 1, and simi+1:T +h = exp\nlarly for the market.\nP s\ns Ri I{Rm < C}\n5. Estimate LRMESiT = − P\ns I{Rm < C}\nShortcut (the paper): simulate market paths first, keep those meeting the crash, then simulate firm innovations only on those paths. Why it differs from the static formula. Resampled residuals keep fat tails, and the filters carry volatility clustering and time-varying correlation from the current state. My simplified GARCH(1,1) simulation (market only, constant beta 1.5, ω = 10−6 , α = 0.08, βG = 0.90, idiosyncratic volatility 1.2% a day, h = 22) shows the state dependence: Start volatility (multiple of long-run) Daily volatility (%) LRMES (%) P (crash) (%) 1.0\n\n0.71\n\n16.1\n\n0.21\n\n1.5\n\n1.06\n\n17.4\n\n1.49\n\n2.0\n\n1.41\n\n18.2\n\n4.30\n\nTable 39.3. A GARCH(1,1) LRMES simulation, 2 × 105 paths; my toy version of Appendix A (no DCC, normal innovations). Both the conditional LRMES and the probability of the event rise when volatility starts high.\n\nLRMES (%)\n\nP(event)=0.2%\n\nP(event)=1.5%\n\nP(event)=4.3%\n\n1.0×\n\n1.5×\nStarting volatility (multiple of long-run level)\n\n2.0×\n\nFigure 39.3. LRMES and crash probability for three starting volatilities (Table 39.3)."}, {"pdf": 289, "printed": 262, "ch": 39, "text": "39.4 What the authors claim empirically (not verified by me)\nFrom the abstract and introduction: SRISK ranks Fannie Mae, Freddie Mac, Morgan Stanley, Bear Stearns and Lehman Brothers among the top contributors as early as 2005–Q1; aggregate SRISK predicts industrial production and unemployment, and predicts Federal Reserve capital injections; and the GARCH–DCC approach “strikes a good balance” for estimating LRMES. Related measures named in the paper: CoVaR, SES and CATFIN. I did not read the tests that support these claims. Do you need the original? For the definition, the derivation of (1) and the algorithm: no. Open the paper (printed pp. 48–79) before quoting the rankings or the predictive results, and Appendix B if you use the Patton copula. Note that the original defines LRMES with arithmetic returns and simulates with GARCH–DCC; Theorem 39.2 is my simplified closed form.\n\n39.5 What it means for the thesis (my inference)\n1. SRISK needs listed equity. Indian public sector banks are listed, so market capitalisation and daily returns exist, which makes SRISK feasible with NSE data.\n2. Two climate channels. A climate event can enter through the market return in the crash (change C or the volatility regime) and through the bank’s own LRMES (a higher beta to a climate factor). Both are my suggestions; the paper does not treat climate.\n3. Government ownership matters. The assumption that debt is not renegotiated in a crash fits poorly with implicit sovereign support; for state-owned banks, SRISK is a measure of the shortfall before any recapitalisation, which is itself the policy-relevant object.\n4. Book versus market leverage. Use consistent book debt and market equity, as the paper does; PSB market caps trade at discounts to book, which raises LVG (my inference).\n\nCheck yourself\nRecall\nQ39.1 Write SRISK in debt–equity form and leverage form.\nQ39.2 What are k, C and h in the paper’s application?\nExplain\nQ39.3 Why is SRISK a measure of capital shortfall rather than distress probability?\nQ39.4 Why does the paper sum only positive SRISKs?\nApply\nQ39.5 D = 300, W = 10, LRMES = 18%, k = 8%. Compute SRISK.\nQ39.6 Market daily volatility 1.5%, h = 22, crash −10%. Compute z and the probability of"}, {"pdf": 290, "printed": 263, "ch": 39, "text": "the event under the normal model.\nQ39.7 Using Theorem 39.2, find the static LRMES for β = 1.2 at σd = 1%.\nViva\nQ39.8 Prove the second form of (1).\nQ39.9 The paper assumes debt is not renegotiable. How does the assumption affect SRISK, and when is it unrealistic?\n\nAnswer sketches\nA39.1 kD − (1 − k)W (1 − LRMES) = W [k LVG + (1 − k)LRMES − 1].\nA39.2 k = 8%, C = −10%, h = 1 month (22 days).\nA39.3 It is the expected amount of capital a firm would need to raise, a balance-sheet quantity, conditional on the crash.\nA39.4 A surplus at one firm cannot recapitalise another.\nA39.5 24 − 0.92 × 10 × 0.82 = 24 − 7.544 = 16.46.\nA39.6 σh = 1.5% 22 = 7.04%, c = ln 0.9 = −0.1054, z = −1.497, P = Φ(−1.497) ≈ 6.7%.\nA39.7 β σh φ(z)/Φ(z) = 1.2 × 0.12163 = 14.6% (using β = 1 value 12.16%).\nA39.8 See the proof of Theorem 39.1.\nA39.9 With renegotiation (debt haircuts), equity absorbs less of the loss and the shortfall falls; it is unrealistic in resolution regimes or under government guarantees."}, {"pdf": 291, "printed": 264, "ch": 40, "text": "CHAPTER 40\n\nThe NGFS Scenarios: What a Climate\nStress Test Takes as Input\nSource line — read first\nNot in your folder. The NGFS (Network for Greening the Financial System)\nscenario material is not among the files I was given.\nI read one public document through the web: the NGFS Phase V scenario outreach presentation (file 20241108_ngfs_scenarios_phasev_outreach_public_v2.pdf, hosted on ngfs.net under January 2025). It is a presentation summary, not the technical documentation. I did not read the NGFS technical documentation, the scenario explorer data, or the earlier phases. The document does not state temperature, carbon prices or model assignments for every scenario; where it is silent I say so. Verify every figure against NGFS before citing it.\n\n40.1 What the NGFS scenarios are\nThe NGFS publishes a common set of long-run climate scenarios for central banks and supervisors. Each scenario is built by an integrated assessment model (IAM) to give emissions, energy-system, land-use and carbon-price paths, and then by a macroeconomic model (NiGEM) to translate them into GDP, inflation, interest rates and sectoral effects. The document names the following models: REMIND-MAgPIE and NiGEM for the scenarios it labels, and it also refers to GCAM 6.0 and MESSAGEix-GLOBIOM 2.0 as IAMs without saying which scenario uses which.\n\n40.2 The scenario set (Phase V)\nThe three families have a clear logic. This is my paraphrase; the presentation states only that disorderly scenarios have higher transition risk and that Current Policies carries high physical risk:\n• Orderly: early, smooth policy; low transition risk; low physical risk.\n• Disorderly: late or uneven action; higher transition risk because carbon prices must rise faster or the world fragments.\n• Hot house: little new policy; low transition risk but high physical risk."}, {"pdf": 292, "printed": 265, "ch": 40, "text": "Scenario\n\nCategory in the What the document says\ndocument’s diagram\n\nNet Zero 2050\n\nOrderly\n\nLimits warming to 1.5 ◦ C; requires steep shadow carbon\nprices (chart axis to US$800/t CO2 ; no single value stated)\n\nBelow 2 ◦ C\n\nOrderly (diagram)\n\nNo numbers in the extract I read\n\nDelayed Transition\n\nDisorderly\ngram)\n\n(dia- Warming below 2 ◦ C; carbon prices higher than in orderly\nscenarios (chart axis to US$400/t CO2 )\n\nFragmented World\n\nDisorderly\ngram)\n\n(dia- No numbers in the extract I read\n\nCurrent Policies\n\nHot house\n(diagram)\n\nworld About 3 ◦ C warming; carbon\nUS$12/t CO2 ; high physical risk\n\nNDCs;\nmand\n\nLow\n\nprice\n\nchart\n\naxis\n\nDe- Not stated in the Named among the scenarios\nextract\n\nTable 40.1. NGFS Phase V scenarios as described in the outreach presentation. Categories are those of the framework diagram; carbon-price figures are chart axis ranges, not reported values.\n\n40.3 Physical and transition risk\nThe presentation reports, for physical risk:\n• Acute losses from four perils exceed 8% of global GDP under Current Policies and remain below 4% under Net Zero 2050.\n• Chronic losses under Current Policies reach about 15% of global GDP by 2050.\n• The text states that, in all scenarios, physical risk rapidly outweighs transition effort. Tipping points are not represented.\n\n40.4 What changed from Phase IV\n• New damage function. Phase V adopts the damage function of Kotz et al. (2024), which adds drivers beyond mean temperature (for example rainfall and temperature variability) and allows growth effects to persist up to ten years.\n• Chronic losses are much larger. For Current Policies, chronic losses in 2050 rise from about 5% (Phase IV) to about 15% (Phase V); the document attributes only 1.5 percentage points of that change to differences in the temperature pathway — most of the rise comes from the damage function.\n• No summing. Chronic and acute risks are no longer added, because that may doublecount.\n• Acute hazards are unchanged; socioeconomic drivers are SSP3.0; policy commitments updated to March 2024; updated IAM versions."}, {"pdf": 293, "printed": 266, "ch": 40, "text": "Current Policies: chronic loss\n% of global GDP (acute, 4 perils)\n\n% of global GDP, 2050\n\nAcute losses: bounds quoted\n\n≈15%\n\n15.0\n12.5\n10.0\n7.5\n≈5%\n\n5.0\n2.5\n0.0\n\nPhase IV\n\nPhase V\n\n> 8%\n\n< 4%\n\nNet Zero 2050\n(below)\n\nCurrent Policies\n(above)\n\nFigure 40.1. Left: chronic physical losses in 2050 under Current Policies, Phase IV versus Phase V. Right: the acute-loss bounds quoted for the two scenarios (they are thresholds, not point estimates). Numbers from the outreach presentation, as I read them.\n\n40.5 How a scenario becomes bank-level input\nNGFS scenarios stop at the macro and sector level; the supervisory exercise has to translate them. The chain, in my words: Step\n\nOutput\n\nPhase 6 chapter\n\n1. NGFS scenario\n\nGDP, inflation, rates, carbon prices, sec- This chapter\ntor output\n\n2. Sector translation Sector PD uplift (or change in distance 37\nto default)\n3. Collateral transla- Asset-price index, LTV, LGD\ntion\n\n4.\nBalance-sheet NPLs, provisions, profit, CET1\nmodule\n\n38, 42\n\n5. Market view\n\nCrash probability, LRMES, SRISK\n\nTable 40.2. From NGFS to capital (my framework, combining the Phase 6 readings).\n\nDo you need the original?\nYes, for anything you cite. Download the NGFS technical documentation and the\nPhase V scenario data (the scenario explorer), choose the scenario(s) and pull the Indian series you need. This chapter only gives you the structure and the numbers from one presentation. I cannot tell you which IAM drives which scenario or what the carbon prices are.\n\n40.6 What it means for the thesis (my inference)\n1. A natural scenario trio: Net Zero 2050 (orderly), Delayed Transition (disorderly) and Current Policies (hot house). They span low and high transition risk and low and high physical risk."}, {"pdf": 294, "printed": 267, "ch": 40, "text": "2. Be explicit about which risk you model. The NGFS notes that physical risk dominates in the long run; a thesis on transition risk should say that it isolates one channel.\n3. Phase V raised chronic physical damages sharply. If a prior study used Phase IV numbers, results will not be comparable to Phase V. State the NGFS phase used.\n4. The macro-to-bank step is yours. NGFS gives no bank PDs. Chapters 37–38 indicate the structure, but the mapping from sectoral output to Indian PSB borrowers is an empirical contribution.\n\nCheck yourself\nRecall\nQ40.1 Name the three scenario families and one scenario in each.\nQ40.2 Which damage function did Phase V adopt?\nExplain\nQ40.3 Why is transition risk higher in disorderly scenarios?\nQ40.4 Why were chronic and acute risks no longer summed?\nApply\nQ40.5 Choose three scenarios for a transition-risk chapter and justify them.\nQ40.6 Chronic losses under Current Policies rose from 5% to 15%. Of the 10-point rise, how much does NGFS attribute to the temperature pathway? Viva\nQ40.7 Why can an “orderly” scenario still hurt a carbon-intensive bank?\nQ40.8 What does the absence of tipping points imply for the interpretation of the results?\n\nAnswer sketches\nA40.1 Orderly (Net Zero 2050), disorderly (Delayed Transition, Fragmented World), hot house (Current Policies).\nA40.2 Kotz et al. (2024).\nA40.3 Delayed or uneven action requires faster, steeper carbon-price rises later.\nA40.4 The two may double-count the same losses.\nA40.5 Net Zero 2050, Delayed Transition, Current Policies: covers low, high and minimal transition effort.\nA40.6 1.5 percentage points.\nA40.7 Transition costs fall on carbon-intensive sectors even when the path is smooth.\nA40.8 Catastrophic outcomes are excluded, so losses are probably understated in the tail."}, {"pdf": 295, "printed": 268, "ch": 41, "text": "CHAPTER 41\n\nClimate Value at Risk of Global Financial Assets (Dietz, Bowen, Dixon and\nGradwell, 2016)\nSource line — read first\nNot in your folder. I read the open-access LSE repository version of Dietz, S., Bowen, A., Dixon, C. and Gradwell, P. (2016), “Climate value at risk of global financial assets”, Nature Climate Change 6: 676–679, through a web fetch that returned a structured summary rather than the full text. Figures below are the ones that summary gave; I could not check the paper’s figures, equations or supplementary information myself. The toy calculation in Section 41.4 is mine. Verify all numbers in the paper before citing.\n\n41.1 What a “climate VaR” is here\nUnlike a bank’s VaR (a quantile of next-period loss), Dietz et al. define Climate VaR as the probability distribution of the present market value of losses on global financial assets caused by climate change, accumulated to 2100. Three features set it apart:\n• It counts only climate impacts (adaptation costs and residual damages), not mitigation costs.\n• It assumes the future damage is not already priced into asset values.\n• Bonds are included using the same framework, by assuming debt and equity are perfect substitutes (a Modigliani–Miller condition).\n\n41.2 The model\nThe base is an extended version of Nordhaus’s DICE-2010, a simple integrated assessment model. Damages depend on warming above pre-industrial levels through a quadratic term plus a random higher-order term to represent catastrophic impacts around 4 ◦ C or more; 0.3 of damages fall on capital (the rest on output). The uncertainty is explored by Monte Carlo with 50 000 Latin hypercube draws over four independent inputs: Discounting. The initial real discount rate is 4.07% (from historical equity and bond returns with a 1.3:1 bond-to-equity weighting), 0.36 percentage points above DICE’s initial GDP growth rate of 3.71%, and then pegged to the DICE growth path. The financial-asset base is about $143 trillion (non-bank financial assets, FSB 2013)."}, {"pdf": 296, "printed": 269, "ch": 41, "text": "Uncertain input\n\nDistribution (as reported)\n\nProductivity (TFP) growth Normal, mean 0.84% a year, standard deviation 0.59%\nPearson Type-V, best-fit mean 2.9 ◦ C, floor of 0.75\n\nClimate sensitivity\n\nDamage-function curvature Uniform on 0 to about 0.248\nBackstop abatement cost\n\nNormal, mean about $343 per tCO2 , standard deviation about $137\n\nTable 41.1. The four Monte Carlo inputs of the Climate VaR model.\n\nFormally. For each draw ω the model yields a path of damages ∆t (ω) to asset income; the loss is the present value relative to a no-climate-damage path, P V assets with climate damages CVaR(ω) = 1 − P V assets without and the reported statistics are the mean and upper percentiles of this distribution across draws (my formalisation of the paper’s description).\n\n41.3 Results (business as usual, 2015–2100)\nExpected 95th percentile 99th percentile\nBusiness as usual (% of asset value)\nBusiness as usual ($ trillion)\n\n1.8\n\n4.8\n\n16.9\n\nabout 2.5\n\nabout 24.2\n\n1.2\n\n2.9\n\n9.2\n\nabout 1.7\n\nabout 13.2\n\n0.6\n\n1.8\n\n7.7\n\n2 ◦ C mitigation (%)\n\n2 C mitigation ($ trillion)\nReduction from mitigation (points)\n\nClimate VaR (% of asset value)\n\nTable 41.2. Climate VaR as reported. Expected warming in 2100 under business as usual is about\n2.5 ◦ C. The 2 ◦ C case is the mitigation scenario with two-thirds probability of staying below 2 ◦ C.\n\n16.9\n\nLognormal fitted to mean and 95th\nBusiness as usual\n2°C mitigation\n\n9.2\n\n4.8\n\n2.9\n1.8\n\n1.2\n\nExpected\n\n95th\n\n99th\n\nFigure 41.1. Climate VaR from Table 41.2. The black marker shows the 99th percentile of a lognormal distribution fitted to the BAU mean and 95th percentile (Section 41.4)."}, {"pdf": 297, "printed": 270, "ch": 41, "text": "Including mitigation costs. Net of the cost of mitigation, the expected present value of assets under the 2 ◦ C path is 0.22% higher than business as usual; at the 99th percentile it is 9.11% higher; about 65% of the distribution lies below zero (as reported). Sensitivities. With a normally distributed damage curvature the BAU expected value falls to 1.46% and the 99th percentile to 9.58%; with a 7% initial discount rate the expected value is 0.96% and the 99th percentile 7.72%. The paper’s variance analysis finds climate sensitivity the most influential input for the expected value, followed by initial productivity growth and damage curvature.\n\n41.4 A toy calculation: how fat is the tail?\nFit a lognormal to the BAU mean (1.8) and 95th percentile (4.8). For X = eµ+σZ the mean is eµ+σ /2 and the 95th percentile is eµ+1.645σ , so \u0001 4.8 q95 = exp 1.645σ − σ 2 /2 = = 2.667. mean 1.8 Solving gives σ = 0.782, µ = 0.282, and a 99th percentile of eµ+2.326σ = 8.2%. The paper’s 99th percentile is 16.9%, twice as large. The tail is fatter than lognormal: the catastrophic-damage term raises the extreme percentiles faster than a distribution calibrated at the centre would predict. (This is my toy comparison; a lognormal is not the model’s distribution.) Result 41.1 (Why the choice of percentile matters). For a lognormal with parameter σ, qp /mean = exp(σzp − σ 2 /2), which is increasing in zp . Hence ranking scenarios by the expected value and ranking them by the 99th percentile can disagree when the scenarios have different dispersion σ.\n\nProof. The expression is the stated ratio since mean = eµ+σ /2 and qp = eµ+σzp . For two scenarios with equal means but σ1 < σ2 , the ratio at a high p (large zp ) is larger for σ2 whenever zp > (σ1 + σ2 )/2 (the exponent difference is (σ2 − σ1 ) zp − (σ1 + σ2 )/2 ).\n\n41.5 Limitations stated by the authors (as summarised)\n• Mitigation costs are excluded from the climate VaR itself.\n• The tail depends heavily on subjective damage-curvature assumptions that lack an evidential basis.\n• Non-market impacts (health, ecosystems) are not fully captured.\n• Hedging may be hard given the global incidence and long holding periods.\n• Modigliani–Miller conditions are assumed; DICE’s 2005 start year is treated as 2015 for present values.\n• The authors were employed by Vivid Economics Ltd during the research."}, {"pdf": 298, "printed": 271, "ch": 41, "text": "Do you need the original?\nYes, if you cite any number or method detail. Download the paper (open access\nthrough the LSE repository) and read the Methods and Supplementary Information; I only had a structured summary. You can use this chapter for orientation: what Climate VaR is, how the Monte Carlo is built and why the tail is so heavy.\n\n41.6 What it means for the thesis (my inference)\n1. It is a long-horizon asset-value measure, not a bank-capital measure. It values global financial assets to 2100; your thesis needs a 3–10 year bank balance-sheet horizon. Treat it as context, not as a model to replicate.\n2. It supports the tail-risk argument. The expected loss (1.8%) is small beside the 99th percentile (16.9%). A regulator calibrating capital to the mean would miss most of the risk — the same logic as VaR versus expected loss in Phase 5.\n3. It excludes transition cost. Climate VaR counts damages only. A thesis on transition risk is on the other side of the ledger: the mitigation costs that Dietz et al. leave out are the stranded-asset and carbon-price losses you study.\n4. Discount-rate sensitivity is a warning. The 99th percentile falls from 16.9% to 7.72% with a 7% initial discount rate; any long-horizon valuation depends on this choice, so report it.\n\nCheck yourself\nRecall\nQ41.1 Define Climate VaR as used by Dietz et al.\nQ41.2 List the four uncertain inputs.\nExplain\nQ41.3 Why are mitigation costs excluded from the climate VaR?\nQ41.4 Why do the authors worry about the damage-curvature assumption?\nApply\nQ41.5 In dollars, what is the expected BAU climate VaR if assets are $143 trillion and the share is 1.8%?\nQ41.6 Show that a lognormal fitted at the mean and 95th percentile gives a 99th percentile of about 8.2%.\nQ41.7 By how many points does mitigation reduce the 99th percentile?\nViva\nQ41.8 Explain why the expected value of Climate VaR understates the case for mitigation."}, {"pdf": 299, "printed": 272, "ch": 41, "text": "Q41.9 How would you reconcile Climate VaR (damages) with a transition-risk stress test (mitigation cost)?\n\nAnswer sketches\nA41.1 The distribution of the present value of climate-caused losses on global financial assets, to 2100.\nA41.2 TFP growth, climate sensitivity, damage-function curvature, backstop abatement cost.\nA41.3 The measure isolates climate impacts; mitigation cost is evaluated separately.\nA41.4 The tail depends on it, and the authors say it lacks an evidential basis.\nA41.5 0.018 × 143 = $2.6 trillion; the paper reports about $2.5 trillion; the small gap is rounding or a difference in the base that I could not check.\nA41.6 Solve exp(1.645σ − σ 2 /2) = 2.667 for σ = 0.782; exp(0.282 + 2.326 × 0.782) = 8.2.\nA41.7 16.9 − 9.2 = 7.7 points.\nA41.8 Most of the gain from mitigation is in the tail (7.7 points at the 99th against 0.6 at the mean).\nA41.9 They are two halves of the same net cost: damages avoided against costs incurred; a combined analysis compares both, as the paper’s net-of-mitigation row does."}, {"pdf": 300, "printed": 273, "ch": 42, "text": "CHAPTER 42\n\nSynthesis: From a Climate Scenario to\na Bank’s Capital Ratio\n42.1 The one-page map\nSource\n\nKey idea\n\nOutput\n\nChapter\n\nSTAMP€ frame- Scenario → satellites → CET1 path; contagion\nwork\nbalance sheet → feedback\n\nSTAMP€ PD\n\nSTAMP€\nand LGD\n\nLogit ADL averaged over Bank PD in (0, 1)\nmany models; D-shift\n\nNPL Stock-flow NPLs;\nratio LGD\n\nsales- NPL ratio, LGD\n\nSRISK\n\nExpected capital shortfall SRISK, LRMES\nin a market crash\n\nNGFS\n\nOrderly, disorderly,\nhouse scenarios\n\nClimate VaR\n\nMonte Carlo over a small Distribution of PV loss to 41\nIAM\n2100\n\nhot- Macro and sector paths\n\nTable 42.1. Six sources and what each delivers.\n\n42.2 The logic that links the chapters\n1. Prerequisites (Ch. 35). Conditional versus unconditional risk; the probit shift; truncated normals; the LRM; GARCH; the shortfall identity.\n2. Scenario (Ch. 40). NGFS supplies macro and sector paths; Dietz et al. (Ch. 41) show why the tail, not the mean, carries the risk.\n3. Credit channel (Ch. 37–38). Scenario → sector PD → bank PD by the D-shift → NPLs and losses; collateral values → LGD.\n4. Capital (Ch. 36). Losses pass through profit to CET1 over RWA.\n5. Market view (Ch. 39). In parallel, SRISK asks what a market crash does to the bank’s capital.\n\n42.3 One chain of numbers, end to end (my illustration)\nA hypothetical public sector bank: loans 100, of which N0 = 8 non-performing; CET1 capital 8.8; RWA 80, so CET1/RWA is 11.0%. Pre-provision profit is 1.2 a year (my assumption, no"}, {"pdf": 301, "printed": 274, "ch": 42, "text": "dividends, no loan growth), LGD is 45% on new defaults, write-off rate 5%, cure 2% of NPLs, starting portfolio PD 2% (D0 = 2.054). The climate scenario is an invented distance-to-default shock: ∆D = −0.25, −0.50, −0.35 in years 1–3. It is not an NGFS output. Baseline Year\n\nPD (%)\n\nClimate stress\n\n2.00 2.00 2.00 3.56 6.01 4.42\n\nCredit loss (new defaults × LGD) 0.83 0.81 0.80 1.48 2.40 1.67\nNPL ratio (%)\n\n9.3 10.5 11.6 10.8 15.5 18.3\n\nCET1 capital\n\n9.17 9.56 9.96 8.52 7.32 6.85\n\nCET1 / RWA (%)\n\n11.5 12.1 12.6 10.7\n\n9.2\n\n8.7\n\nTable 42.2. A complete pipeline in 3 years. Every number is mine and the parameters are invented; the point is the structure. RWA = 0.8× total loans.\n\nBaseline\nStress\n\nAnnual PD (%)\n\nCET1 / RWA (%)\n\nBaseline\nClimate stress\n\nillustrative 8% reference\n\n0.0\n\n0.5\n\n1.0\n\n1.5\nYear\n\n2.0\n\n2.5\n\n3.0\n\nFigure 42.1. Left: CET1 over RWA under baseline and stress, with an illustrative 8% reference line. Right: the annual PDs. The stress path ends 3.9 percentage points below the baseline path.\n\nWhat the chain shows. (i) A modest shock to distance to default (−0.50 at its peak) takes a 2% PD to 6%. (ii) NPLs react more than PDs because write-offs and cures are slow. (iii) The difference in capital is about 3.9 points of RWA by year 3. (iv) The result depends on the invented shock, PPP, LGD and RWA rules; it demonstrates a method and is not a forecast of any bank.\n\n42.4 Ninety-minute lecture outline\n42.5 Test\nShort answers (4 marks each).\n1. List the four pillars of STAMP€ and say where the climate scenario enters.\n2. Why is the D-shift preferred to multiplying PDs?"}, {"pdf": 302, "printed": 275, "ch": 42, "text": "Minutes\n\nBlock\n\nContent\n\n0–5\n\nThe question\n\nWhat is a climate stress test? Conditional versus unconditional\n\n5–20\n\nFramework\n\nFour pillars; Figure 36.1; Table 36.2\n\n20–40\n\nPD and the D-shift\n\nTheorem 35.1; Table 37.2; model averaging\n\n40–55\n\nNPLs and LGD\n\nTable 38.1; sales ratio; Table 38.2\n\n55–70\n\nSRISK\n\nTheorem 39.1; Tables 39.1, 39.2\n\n70–80\n\nScenarios\nVaR\n\n80–90\n\nClose\n\nand\n\nClimate Table 40.1; Table 41.2; the toy lognormal\nTable 42.2 and the thesis question\n\nTable 42.3. A 90-minute lecture.\n\n3. State the NPL stock-flow equation and explain the cure term.\n4. Write SRISK in leverage form and say when it is positive.\n5. Name two things Climate VaR leaves out.\nLonger questions (10 marks each).\n6. Derive the closed-form sales ratio and the LGD formula; explain how a house-price fall changes LGD.\n7. Prove the LRM formula for an ADL model and explain how it is used in the PD models.\n8. Derive the static LRMES and compare it with the simulation of Appendix A.\n9. Explain Table 41.2 and the claim that the tail is fatter than lognormal.\n10. Design a climate stress test for an Indian public sector bank using the chain of Table 42.2; state which inputs are estimated and which are assumed.\n\nAnswer keys\nA42.1 Scenario, satellites, balance sheet, feedback; the climate scenario is the first pillar’s input.\nA42.2 It keeps PDs inside (0, 1) and has the right tail behaviour (Theorem 35.1, Theorem 37.1).\nA42.3 Nt = Nt−1 (1 − w) + PDPt−1 − Cure; cure returns loans to performing.\nA42.4 W [k LVG + (1 − k)LRMES − 1]; positive when the bracket is positive.\nA42.5 Mitigation costs; non-market impacts (and others in the limitations list).\nA42.6 Theorem 35.3 and Chapter 38.\nA42.7 Theorem 35.4.\nA42.8 Theorem 39.2; Appendix A steps 1–5 (Chapter 39).\nA42.9 Chapter 41, including Section 41.4.\nA42.10 The chain of this chapter: estimated (NPL history, balance sheet, equity returns), assumed (scenario shock, PPP, LGD, cure)."}, {"pdf": 303, "printed": 276, "ch": 42, "text": "42.6 What is verified, what is not\nSource\n\nStatus\n\nNotes\n\nSTAMP€ Chapters 2–4\n\nRead\n\nChapter 3 numbers as I read them from extracted text; GDP\nranges to be verified; LGL formula not shown in the extract\n\nSTAMP€ Chapters 5– Not studied\n\nLoan flows, DSGE and GVAR, contagion\n\nBrownlees\n(2017)\n\nDefinition, LRMES and Appendix A read; empirical sections\nand Appendix B not read\n\nand\n\nEngle Partly read\n\nNGFS Phase V\n\nOne\npublic Not the technical documentation; no scenario-by-model aspresentation\nsignments or carbon-price values\n\nDietz et al. (2016)\n\nWeb summary Figures not checked against the paper\nonly\n\nAll numerical tables, Mine\nsimulations and the\npipeline\n\nSales-ratio formula verified by Monte Carlo; LRMES by\nMonte Carlo; pipeline parameters invented\n\nTable 42.4. Provenance of Phase 6."}], "cards": [{"id": "35.1", "ch": 35, "lv": "Recall", "q": "State Theorem 35.2 and Theorem 35.4.", "a": "E(Z | Z < z) = −φ(z)/Φ(z); LRM = P βj /(1 − P ρi )."}, {"id": "35.2", "ch": 35, "lv": "Recall", "q": "Write CS in debt–equity form and leverage form.", "a": "CS = kD − (1 − k)W = W (k LVG − 1). P"}, {"id": "35.3", "ch": 35, "lv": "Explain", "q": "Why is a stress-test number not a risk measure?", "a": "It is one conditional term in E(L) = P (s)E(L | s); P (s) is not given."}, {"id": "35.4", "ch": 35, "lv": "Explain", "q": "Why does shifting in D keep PDs inside [0, 1]?", "a": "Φ maps R onto (0, 1), so any real shift gives a valid probability."}, {"id": "35.5", "ch": 35, "lv": "Apply", "q": "p = 2%, ∆ = −0.5. Compute p′ .", "a": "D = 2.054; ∆ = −0.5 gives D′ = 1.554 and p′ = Φ(−1.554) = 6.0%.    "}, {"id": "35.6", "ch": 35, "lv": "Apply", "q": "K = 10, L = 4. How many equations?", "a": "10 1 + 2 + 3 + 4 = 10 + 45 + 120 + 210 = 385."}, {"id": "35.7", "ch": 35, "lv": "Apply", "q": "ω = 2 × 10−6 , α = 0.07, β = 0.91. Find the long-run daily volatility.", "a": "σ 2 = 2 × 10−6 /0.02 = 10−4 , so σ = 1% per day. Rz"}, {"id": "35.8", "ch": 35, "lv": "Viva", "q": "Prove E(Z | Z < z) = −φ(z)/Φ(z).", "a": "−∞ uφ(u)du = −φ(z) since φ′ = −uφ; divide by Φ(z)."}, {"id": "35.9", "ch": 35, "lv": "Viva", "q": "Explain why a large permanent shock to x has a bigger effect on y in the long run than at impact.", "a": "The lagged dependent variable propagates the effect: 1/(1 − ρ) > 1 when 0 < ρ < 1 and β’s have one sign."}, {"id": "36.1", "ch": 36, "lv": "Recall", "q": "Name the four pillars and the seven satellite models.", "a": "Scenario design; satellite models; balance sheet; feedback. Satellites: PD, LGD, spreads, NIM, market risk, fees, operational risk."}, {"id": "36.2", "ch": 36, "lv": "Recall", "q": "What happened to the CET1 ratio in the 2016 application?", "a": "From 13.0% to 9.1%, about 390 bp, for 37 banks."}, {"id": "36.3", "ch": 36, "lv": "Explain", "q": "Why does a stress test need a balance-sheet module as well as a PD model?", "a": "PDs are not capital; losses must pass through provisions, profit, RWA and retained earnings."}, {"id": "36.4", "ch": 36, "lv": "Explain", "q": "Why is contagion shown separately from the direct effect?", "a": "The direct channel is a first-round effect; contagion is conditional on bank interlinkages and has a fat tail (median 6 bp vs 12 bp at the 90th percentile)."}, {"id": "36.5", "ch": 36, "lv": "Apply", "q": "A bank starts at 13.0% CET1 and falls by 390 bp. What is the end ratio, and by what proportion of the starting ratio did it fall?", "a": "13.0 − 3.9 = 9.1%; 3.9/13.0 = 30%."}, {"id": "36.6", "ch": 36, "lv": "Apply", "q": "Which pillar would you change to introduce a carbon tax, and which one stays the same?", "a": "The scenario module (pillar 1); the balance-sheet identities (pillar 3) are unchanged."}, {"id": "36.7", "ch": 36, "lv": "Viva", "q": "The report contains no climate scenario. Defend the claim that it is still useful for a climate-risk thesis.", "a": "The pillars are generic: scenario → risk drivers → capital. A climate scenario is another input to pillar 1."}, {"id": "36.8", "ch": 36, "lv": "Viva", "q": "What can a partial-equilibrium stress test not tell you?", "a": "It cannot say how banks’ deleveraging changes GDP, or how losses spread between banks."}, {"id": "37.1", "ch": 37, "lv": "Recall", "q": "List the six PD segments and the data source for financials and sovereigns.", "a": "NFC-RE, NFC non-RE, HH-HP, HH-CC, FIN, SOV; Moody’s KMV for financials, Kamakura for sovereigns. A37.2 P βj /(1 − P ρi ); multiplied by sd(X)/sd(Y )."}, {"id": "37.2", "ch": 37, "lv": "Recall", "q": "Define the long-run multiplier and the normalised LRM.", "a": "The model answer is a formula that does not extract cleanly. Open the printed page to read it."}, {"id": "37.3", "ch": 37, "lv": "Explain", "q": "Why a logit for the default rate?", "a": "It maps (0, 1) to R, so fitted rates lie in (0, 1)."}, {"id": "37.4", "ch": 37, "lv": "Explain", "q": "Why impose sign restrictions after estimating?", "a": "Many specifications fit the short sample; restrictions remove those with implausible signs."}, {"id": "37.5", "ch": 37, "lv": "Apply", "q": "A bank has p0 = 3% and the sector shock is 2% → 5%. Find the stressed bank PD.", "a": "∆D = −Φ−1 (0.05) + Φ−1 (0.02) = 1.645 − 2.054 = −0.409. D0 = 1.881; D1 = 1.472; p1 = Φ(−1.472) = 7.1%."}, {"id": "37.6", "ch": 37, "lv": "Apply", "q": "K = 7, L = 3. How many equations?", "a": "7 + 21 + 35 = 63."}, {"id": "37.7", "ch": 37, "lv": "Apply", "q": "ρ = 0.5, β0 = 0.2, β1 = 0.1. Find the LRM.", "a": "0.3/0.5 = 0.6."}, {"id": "37.8", "ch": 37, "lv": "Viva", "q": "Prove that the D-shift cannot produce a PD above one.", "a": "Φ takes values in (0, 1) for every real argument."}, {"id": "37.9", "ch": 37, "lv": "Viva", "q": "Give two limitations of using sector-level PDs to stress a single bank.", "a": "The sector PD hides the bank’s composition; the shift is the same for banks with very different portfolios; the starting PD is only a proxy."}, {"id": "38.1", "ch": 38, "lv": "Recall", "q": "Write the NPL and performing-loan recursions and interpret each term.", "a": "Nt = Nt−1 (1 − w) + PDPt−1 − Cure; Pt = Pt−1 (1 − r − PD) + NB + Cure. New NPLs leave the performing book; cures return."}, {"id": "38.2", "ch": 38, "lv": "Recall", "q": "State the structural LGD formula.", "a": "LGD = (1 − P (cure)) LGL + Costs."}, {"id": "38.3", "ch": 38, "lv": "Explain", "q": "Why is LGD higher when LTV is higher?", "a": "The loan exceeds a larger share of the collateral value, so recoveries are capped below the loan more often."}, {"id": "38.4", "ch": 38, "lv": "Explain", "q": "What is the difference between indexing and front-loading house prices?", "a": "Indexing moves LTV with the price path each year; front-loading applies the whole cumulative fall at once."}, {"id": "38.5", "ch": 38, "lv": "Apply", "q": "N0 = 8, P0 = 92, w = 5%, Cure = 2%, PD = 4%. Find N1 .", "a": "N1 = 8(0.95) + 0.04(92) − 0.16 = 7.6 + 3.68 − 0.16 = 11.12."}, {"id": "38.6", "ch": 38, "lv": "Apply", "q": "A 25% price fall; starting LTV 0.60. What is the indexed LTV?", "a": "0.60/0.75 = 0.80."}, {"id": "38.7", "ch": 38, "lv": "Apply", "q": "Compute LGD with LGL = 7%, cure 30%, costs 5%.", "a": "0.7 × 0.07 + 0.05 = 0.099, i.e. 9.9%."}, {"id": "38.8", "ch": 38, "lv": "Viva", "q": "Derive the sales-ratio expression from the normal distribution.", "a": "Theorem 35.3: split on S ≤ 0, 0 < S < LTV, S ≥ LTV; use Theorem 35.2."}, {"id": "38.9", "ch": 38, "lv": "Viva", "q": "Why can LGD stay low for moderate price falls and then rise sharply?", "a": "SR′ (x) decreases in x: only when LTV nears the centre of the sales-ratio distribution does extra LTV start losing recovery (Theorem 38.1)."}, {"id": "39.1", "ch": 39, "lv": "Recall", "q": "Write SRISK in debt–equity form and leverage form.", "a": "kD − (1 − k)W (1 − LRMES) = W [k LVG + (1 − k)LRMES − 1]."}, {"id": "39.2", "ch": 39, "lv": "Recall", "q": "What are k, C and h in the paper’s application?", "a": "k = 8%, C = −10%, h = 1 month (22 days)."}, {"id": "39.3", "ch": 39, "lv": "Explain", "q": "Why is SRISK a measure of capital shortfall rather than distress probability?", "a": "It is the expected amount of capital a firm would need to raise, a balance-sheet quantity, conditional on the crash."}, {"id": "39.4", "ch": 39, "lv": "Explain", "q": "Why does the paper sum only positive SRISKs?", "a": "A surplus at one firm cannot recapitalise another."}, {"id": "39.5", "ch": 39, "lv": "Apply", "q": "D = 300, W = 10, LRMES = 18%, k = 8%. Compute SRISK.", "a": "24 − 0.92 × 10 × 0.82 = 24 − 7.544 = 16.46. √"}, {"id": "39.6", "ch": 39, "lv": "Apply", "q": "Market daily volatility 1.5%, h = 22, crash −10%. Compute z and the probability of the event under the normal model.", "a": "σh = 1.5% 22 = 7.04%, c = ln 0.9 = −0.1054, z = −1.497, P = Φ(−1.497) ≈ 6.7%."}, {"id": "39.7", "ch": 39, "lv": "Apply", "q": "Using Theorem 39.2, find the static LRMES for β = 1.2 at σd = 1%.", "a": "β σh φ(z)/Φ(z) = 1.2 × 0.12163 = 14.6% (using β = 1 value 12.16%)."}, {"id": "39.8", "ch": 39, "lv": "Viva", "q": "Prove the second form of (1).", "a": "See the proof of Theorem 39.1."}, {"id": "39.9", "ch": 39, "lv": "Viva", "q": "The paper assumes debt is not renegotiable. How does the assumption affect SRISK, and when is it unrealistic?", "a": "With renegotiation (debt haircuts), equity absorbs less of the loss and the shortfall falls; it is unrealistic in resolution regimes or under government guarantees."}, {"id": "40.1", "ch": 40, "lv": "Recall", "q": "Name the three scenario families and one scenario in each.", "a": "Orderly (Net Zero 2050), disorderly (Delayed Transition, Fragmented World), hot house (Current Policies)."}, {"id": "40.2", "ch": 40, "lv": "Recall", "q": "Which damage function did Phase V adopt?", "a": "Kotz et al. (2024)."}, {"id": "40.3", "ch": 40, "lv": "Explain", "q": "Why is transition risk higher in disorderly scenarios?", "a": "Delayed or uneven action requires faster, steeper carbon-price rises later."}, {"id": "40.4", "ch": 40, "lv": "Explain", "q": "Why were chronic and acute risks no longer summed?", "a": "The two may double-count the same losses."}, {"id": "40.5", "ch": 40, "lv": "Apply", "q": "Choose three scenarios for a transition-risk chapter and justify them.", "a": "Net Zero 2050, Delayed Transition, Current Policies: covers low, high and minimal transition effort."}, {"id": "40.6", "ch": 40, "lv": "Apply", "q": "Chronic losses under Current Policies rose from 5% to 15%. Of the 10-point rise, how much does NGFS attribute to the temperature pathway?", "a": "1.5 percentage points."}, {"id": "40.7", "ch": 40, "lv": "Viva", "q": "Why can an “orderly” scenario still hurt a carbon-intensive bank?", "a": "Transition costs fall on carbon-intensive sectors even when the path is smooth."}, {"id": "40.8", "ch": 40, "lv": "Viva", "q": "What does the absence of tipping points imply for the interpretation of the results?", "a": "Catastrophic outcomes are excluded, so losses are probably understated in the tail."}, {"id": "41.1", "ch": 41, "lv": "Recall", "q": "Define Climate VaR as used by Dietz et al.", "a": "The distribution of the present value of climate-caused losses on global financial assets, to 2100."}, {"id": "41.2", "ch": 41, "lv": "Recall", "q": "List the four uncertain inputs.", "a": "TFP growth, climate sensitivity, damage-function curvature, backstop abatement cost."}, {"id": "41.3", "ch": 41, "lv": "Explain", "q": "Why are mitigation costs excluded from the climate VaR?", "a": "The measure isolates climate impacts; mitigation cost is evaluated separately."}, {"id": "41.4", "ch": 41, "lv": "Explain", "q": "Why do the authors worry about the damage-curvature assumption?", "a": "The tail depends on it, and the authors say it lacks an evidential basis."}, {"id": "41.5", "ch": 41, "lv": "Apply", "q": "In dollars, what is the expected BAU climate VaR if assets are $143 trillion and the share is 1.8%?", "a": "0.018 × 143 = $2.6 trillion; the paper reports about $2.5 trillion; the small gap is rounding or a difference in the base that I could not check."}, {"id": "41.6", "ch": 41, "lv": "Apply", "q": "Show that a lognormal fitted at the mean and 95th percentile gives a 99th percentile of about 8.2%.", "a": "Solve exp(1.645σ − σ 2 /2) = 2.667 for σ = 0.782; exp(0.282 + 2.326 × 0.782) = 8.2."}, {"id": "41.7", "ch": 41, "lv": "Apply", "q": "By how many points does mitigation reduce the 99th percentile?", "a": "16.9 − 9.2 = 7.7 points."}, {"id": "41.8", "ch": 41, "lv": "Viva", "q": "Explain why the expected value of Climate VaR understates the case for mitigation.", "a": "Most of the gain from mitigation is in the tail (7.7 points at the 99th against 0.6 at the mean)."}, {"id": "41.9", "ch": 41, "lv": "Viva", "q": "How would you reconcile Climate VaR (damages) with a transition-risk stress test (mitigation cost)?", "a": "They are two halves of the same net cost: damages avoided against costs incurred; a combined analysis compares both, as the paper’s net-of-mitigation row does."}, {"id": "42.1", "ch": 42, "lv": "Short (4 marks)", "q": "List the four pillars of STAMP€ and say where the climate scenario enters.", "a": "Scenario, satellites, balance sheet, feedback; the climate scenario is the first pillar’s input."}, {"id": "42.2", "ch": 42, "lv": "Short (4 marks)", "q": "Why is the D-shift preferred to multiplying PDs?", "a": "It keeps PDs inside (0, 1) and has the right tail behaviour (Theorem 35.1, Theorem 37.1)."}, {"id": "42.3", "ch": 42, "lv": "Short (4 marks)", "q": "State the NPL stock-flow equation and explain the cure term.", "a": "Nt = Nt−1 (1 − w) + PDPt−1 − Cure; cure returns loans to performing."}, {"id": "42.4", "ch": 42, "lv": "Short (4 marks)", "q": "Write SRISK in leverage form and say when it is positive.", "a": "W [k LVG + (1 − k)LRMES − 1]; positive when the bracket is positive."}, {"id": "42.5", "ch": 42, "lv": "Short (4 marks)", "q": "Name two things Climate VaR leaves out.", "a": "Mitigation costs; non-market impacts (and others in the limitations list)."}, {"id": "42.6", "ch": 42, "lv": "Long (10 marks)", "q": "Derive the closed-form sales ratio and the LGD formula; explain how a house-price fall changes LGD.", "a": "Theorem 35.3 and Chapter 38."}, {"id": "42.7", "ch": 42, "lv": "Long (10 marks)", "q": "Prove the LRM formula for an ADL model and explain how it is used in the PD models.", "a": "Theorem 35.4."}, {"id": "42.8", "ch": 42, "lv": "Long (10 marks)", "q": "Derive the static LRMES and compare it with the simulation of Appendix A.", "a": "Theorem 39.2; Appendix A steps 1–5 (Chapter 39)."}, {"id": "42.9", "ch": 42, "lv": "Long (10 marks)", "q": "Explain Table 41.2 and the claim that the tail is fatter than lognormal.", "a": "Chapter 41, including Section 41.4."}, {"id": "42.10", "ch": 42, "lv": "Long (10 marks)", "q": "Design a climate stress test for an Indian public sector bank using the chain of Table 42.2; state which inputs are estimated and which are assumed.", "a": "The chain of this chapter: estimated (NPL history, balance sheet, equity returns), assumed (scenario shock, PPP, LGD, cure)."}]};